export default {"format":"cadcraft-help","version":1,"app":"civilcraft","addon":"watercraft","name":"WaterCraft","sections":{"start":"Getting started","tasks":"Walkthroughs","switching":"Coming from other software","reference":"Reference","trouble":"Troubleshooting and limits"},"topics":[{"slug":"watercraft-scenarios","scope":"addon:watercraft","title":"WaterCraft: scenarios, shortage and leakage, rules, valves and pump energy","summary":"Compare \"what if\" versions of the network side by side, see what a network delivers when the pressure falls, and run a day with control rules, flow-control and pressure-sustaining valves and pump energy.","section":"tasks","order":100,"keywords":["watercraft","scenarios","compare","what if","pressure dependent demand","shortage","leakage","emitter","control rules","valve","flow control valve","pressure sustaining valve","booster","pump energy","kwh","tariff","tank level"],"headings":[{"id":"scenarios-side-by-side","text":"Scenarios side by side","level":2},{"id":"shortage-and-leakage","text":"Shortage and leakage","level":2},{"id":"valves-rules-and-pump-energy","text":"Valves, rules and pump energy","level":2},{"id":"what-it-does-not-do-yet","text":"What it does not do yet","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EThese sections of the WaterCraft tab work on the Water mains already in the project. Add the add-on first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA calculation aid only. Each result below was checked against published results of EPA&#039;s Example Network 1 (and variants of it) and against hand answers, but not against a real utility&#039;s model. A water engineer must check every result.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"scenarios-side-by-side\"\u003EScenarios side by side\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EScenarios side by side\u003C/span\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWrite one scenario per heading: a line \u003Ccode\u003Escenario Peak day\u003C/code\u003E, then its changes, one a line: \u003Ccode\u003Eclose P12\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Eopen P12\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edemand x1.5\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Edemand J5 120\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ediameter P3 16\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ec P4 80\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Epump P1 off\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Etank T1 level 130\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Ereservoir R1 head 820\u003C/code\u003E, \u003Ccode\u003Efire J9 1000\u003C/code\u003E.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The first scenario is the base. Each later one shows its measure and, beside it, the change from the base: the lowest and highest pressure, junctions under the minimum or over the maximum, the fastest pipe, the demand served and cut off, and what is taken from the sources.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ETick \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ELet the pressure decide what is delivered (shortage run)\u003C/span\u003E to run every scenario the shortage way (below).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESave as CSV\u003C/span\u003E for the measures, or \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPressures as CSV\u003C/span\u003E for every junction.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA line that is not understood is named by its number; a pipe or junction that is not in the network is listed, never skipped silently. The scenario text lives in the panel while the page is open: copy it out to keep it.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"shortage-and-leakage\"\u003EShortage and leakage\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EAn ordinary run delivers every demand whatever the pressure. Under \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EShortage and leakage\u003C/span\u003E a junction delivers only what its pressure allows: nothing at or under the lowest pressure you give, everything at or over the full pressure, and a curve between them with the exponent you set (0.5 is usual).\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the two pressures and the exponent, and a demand multiplier if you want a peak.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor leaking joints, write one a line: junction, coefficient. The leak is the coefficient times the pressure to the power 0.5.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor a leak along the pipes, give the flow per 1,000 length units at one pressure unit and the exponent (about 0.5 for holes, up to 1.5 for cracks that open as the pressure rises).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The line shows what was wanted, delivered and short, and what the leaks take; the table lists the junctions that get less than they ask.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"valves-rules-and-pump-energy\"\u003EValves, rules and pump energy\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EValves, rules and pump energy\u003C/span\u003E, write control rules, one a line, in the usual words: \u003Ccode\u003ELINK P1 CLOSED IF NODE T1 ABOVE 140\u003C/code\u003E (a tank level), \u003Ccode\u003ELINK P1 OPEN IF NODE J9 BELOW 30\u003C/code\u003E (a junction pressure), \u003Ccode\u003ELINK P1 OPEN AT TIME 6\u003C/code\u003E (hours into the run) or \u003Ccode\u003ELINK P1 CLOSED AT CLOCKTIME 22\u003C/code\u003E. A tank-level rule is met exactly: the step is cut to the moment the level crosses the value. Pressure rules are checked at step boundaries.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWrite valves, one a line: id, pipe, \u003Ccode\u003EFCV\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003EPSV\u003C/code\u003E, setting. A flow-control valve holds the flow at the setting when the open valve would pass more. A pressure-sustaining valve holds the pressure at its upstream junction: open while that pressure is above the setting, throttled when it would fall under, closed when even no flow does not bring it back. A booster is an ordinary pump on a design point, switched by a rule.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the hours, the step, the pump efficiency, the price per kWh and, if you have one, the price by hour of day. If the demand pattern changes more slowly than the step, give the pattern step too.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The tables show each rule that fired and when, each pump&#039;s energy, average and peak kW, time on and cost, and the tank levels through the day.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"what-it-does-not-do-yet\"\u003EWhat it does not do yet\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003EA throttled valve between two tanks, or into a tank from a junction, is not exact (the tank&#039;s inflow is counted, but two throttled valves in series are settled one after the other).\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ERules and valves are not saved in the project file yet: copy the text out.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPump efficiency is one number a pump, not a curve.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E","text":"These sections of the WaterCraft tab work on the Water mains already in the project. Add the add-on first. A calculation aid only. Each result below was checked against published results of EPA's Example Network 1 (and variants of it) and against hand answers, but not against a real utility's model. A water engineer must check every result. Scenarios side by side Open Scenarios side by side. Write one scenario per heading: a line scenario Peak day, then its changes, one a line: close P12, open P12, demand x1.5, demand J5 120, diameter P3 16, c P4 80, pump P1 off, tank T1 level 130, reservoir R1 head 820, fire J9 1000. Press Run. The first scenario is the base. Each later one shows its measure and, beside it, the change from the base: the lowest and highest pressure, junctions under the minimum or over the maximum, the fastest pipe, the demand served and cut off, and what is taken from the sources. Tick Let the pressure decide what is delivered (shortage run) to run every scenario the shortage way (below). Press Save as CSV for the measures, or Pressures as CSV for every junction. A line that is not understood is named by its number; a pipe or junction that is not in the network is listed, never skipped silently. The scenario text lives in the panel while the page is open: copy it out to keep it. Shortage and leakage An ordinary run delivers every demand whatever the pressure. Under Shortage and leakage a junction delivers only what its pressure allows: nothing at or under the lowest pressure you give, everything at or over the full pressure, and a curve between them with the exponent you set (0.5 is usual). Set the two pressures and the exponent, and a demand multiplier if you want a peak. For leaking joints, write one a line: junction, coefficient. The leak is the coefficient times the pressure to the power 0.5. For a leak along the pipes, give the flow per 1,000 length units at one pressure unit and the exponent (about 0.5 for holes, up to 1.5 for cracks that open as the pressure rises). Press Run. The line shows what was wanted, delivered and short, and what the leaks take; the table lists the junctions that get less than they ask. Valves, rules and pump energy Under Valves, rules and pump energy, write control rules, one a line, in the usual words: LINK P1 CLOSED IF NODE T1 ABOVE 140 (a tank level), LINK P1 OPEN IF NODE J9 BELOW 30 (a junction pressure), LINK P1 OPEN AT TIME 6 (hours into the run) or LINK P1 CLOSED AT CLOCKTIME 22. A tank-level rule is met exactly: the step is cut to the moment the level crosses the value. Pressure rules are checked at step boundaries. Write valves, one a line: id, pipe, FCV or PSV, setting. A flow-control valve holds the flow at the setting when the open valve would pass more. A pressure-sustaining valve holds the pressure at its upstream junction: open while that pressure is above the setting, throttled when it would fall under, closed when even no flow does not bring it back. A booster is an ordinary pump on a design point, switched by a rule. Set the hours, the step, the pump efficiency, the price per kWh and, if you have one, the price by hour of day. If the demand pattern changes more slowly than the step, give the pattern step too. Press Run. The tables show each rule that fired and when, each pump's energy, average and peak kW, time on and cost, and the tank levels through the day. What it does not do yet A throttled valve between two tanks, or into a tank from a junction, is not exact (the tank's inflow is counted, but two throttled valves in series are settled one after the other). Rules and valves are not saved in the project file yet: copy the text out. Pump efficiency is one number a pump, not a curve."},{"slug":"watercraft-studies","scope":"addon:watercraft","title":"WaterCraft: parcels, emergencies, calibration and pipe sizes","summary":"Turn the parcels you drew into demands, run a main break, a fire or a power cut and see how long the tank lasts, fit the pipes' roughness to field readings, and size or rehabilitate pipes for the money.","section":"tasks","order":101,"keywords":["watercraft","parcels","land use","demand","emergency","main break","fire","power cut","tank","calibration","field readings","roughness","hazen williams","sizing","pipe diameter","rehabilitation","budget"],"headings":[{"id":"demands-from-parcels","text":"Demands from parcels","level":2},{"id":"emergencies","text":"Emergencies","level":2},{"id":"calibrate-to-field-readings","text":"Calibrate to field readings","level":2},{"id":"pipe-sizes-and-rehabilitation","text":"Pipe sizes and rehabilitation","level":2}],"html":"\u003Cp\u003EAdd the add-on first.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Caside class=\"hc hc-warning\"\u003E\u003Cp class=\"hc-label\"\u003EWarning\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EA calculation aid only. These were checked against published results of EPA&#039;s Example Network 1 and variants of it, against closed forms (the diameter a pipe needs, a tank emptying at a steady demand) and by hand, not against a real utility&#039;s model. A water engineer must check every result. Prices are yours: the add-on ships no price list.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003C/aside\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"demands-from-parcels\"\u003EDemands from parcels\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EDraw or import parcels in CivilCraft first.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EDemands from parcels\u003C/span\u003E. Say which parcel is which use, one a line as \u003Ccode\u003Eid = use\u003C/code\u003E (uses are the kinds in the demand table: residential, apartment, commercial, industrial, school, park), or pick one use for the rest. A parcel with no use, or no shape, is left out and named.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor homes, give the area of one home (project area units); with 0, each parcel is one home.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ECheck\u003C/span\u003E to see the demand each junction gets (every parcel goes to the nearest junction), then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPut into the network\u003C/span\u003E. Undo takes it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe demands per home and per acre are starting values: use your utility&#039;s own.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"emergencies\"\u003EEmergencies\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EOpen \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EEmergencies\u003C/span\u003E, choose the event and press \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E:\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EMain break\u003C/span\u003E: name the pipe. It leaves service, with the pipes around it that the valves drawn on the network isolate.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EFire at a hydrant\u003C/span\u003E: name the junction, the flow and the hours it runs.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPower cut\u003C/span\u003E: name the pumps (empty = all) and the hours.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\n\u003Cp\u003EThe result is the demand cut off, the junctions under the minimum pressure and for how long, the lowest pressure, and for each tank its level at the start and the end and when it runs empty.\u003C/p\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"calibrate-to-field-readings\"\u003ECalibrate to field readings\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWrite the readings, one a line: \u003Ccode\u003Epressure, J5, 62.4\u003C/code\u003E or \u003Ccode\u003Eflow, P3, 410\u003C/code\u003E, and, if the reading was taken at a busier or quieter time, the demand multiplier as a last value.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EWrite the groups of pipes whose roughness should move together, one a line: \u003Ccode\u003Emains: P1 P2 P3\u003C/code\u003E. None means all pipes together. Give more readings than groups.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EPress \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ERun\u003C/span\u003E. The table shows each group&#039;s roughness C before and after and every reading before and after. A group the readings hardly feel is named, because its answer means little.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EApply the new roughness\u003C/span\u003E writes it into the water mains; Undo puts it back.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E\n\u003Ch2 id=\"pipe-sizes-and-rehabilitation\"\u003EPipe sizes and rehabilitation\u003C/h2\u003E\n\u003Col\u003E\u003Cli\u003EUnder \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EPipe sizes and rehabilitation\u003C/span\u003E, list the sizes you may buy and a price per length unit for each.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003ESet the minimum pressure and the highest velocity.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003ESize the pipes\u003C/span\u003E makes the pipes that help most for the money larger until every junction meets the pressure and no pipe is too fast, then takes back any size that was not needed. No pipe is made smaller. If the sizes you listed cannot meet the limits, it says so.\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003EFor old pipes, name the options (name, new C, price) and a budget, then \u003Cspan class=\"hui\"\u003EChoose pipes to rehabilitate\u003C/span\u003E. The budget is a ceiling, never exceeded; what it cannot reach is reported.\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ol\u003E","text":"Add the add-on first. A calculation aid only. These were checked against published results of EPA's Example Network 1 and variants of it, against closed forms (the diameter a pipe needs, a tank emptying at a steady demand) and by hand, not against a real utility's model. A water engineer must check every result. Prices are yours: the add-on ships no price list. Demands from parcels Draw or import parcels in CivilCraft first. Open Demands from parcels. Say which parcel is which use, one a line as id = use (uses are the kinds in the demand table: residential, apartment, commercial, industrial, school, park), or pick one use for the rest. A parcel with no use, or no shape, is left out and named. For homes, give the area of one home (project area units); with 0, each parcel is one home. Press Check to see the demand each junction gets (every parcel goes to the nearest junction), then Put into the network. Undo takes it back. The demands per home and per acre are starting values: use your utility's own. Emergencies Open Emergencies, choose the event and press Run: Main break: name the pipe. It leaves service, with the pipes around it that the valves drawn on the network isolate. Fire at a hydrant: name the junction, the flow and the hours it runs. Power cut: name the pumps (empty = all) and the hours. The result is the demand cut off, the junctions under the minimum pressure and for how long, the lowest pressure, and for each tank its level at the start and the end and when it runs empty. Calibrate to field readings Write the readings, one a line: pressure, J5, 62.4 or flow, P3, 410, and, if the reading was taken at a busier or quieter time, the demand multiplier as a last value. Write the groups of pipes whose roughness should move together, one a line: mains: P1 P2 P3. None means all pipes together. Give more readings than groups. Press Run. The table shows each group's roughness C before and after and every reading before and after. A group the readings hardly feel is named, because its answer means little. Apply the new roughness writes it into the water mains; Undo puts it back. Pipe sizes and rehabilitation Under Pipe sizes and rehabilitation, list the sizes you may buy and a price per length unit for each. Set the minimum pressure and the highest velocity. Size the pipes makes the pipes that help most for the money larger until every junction meets the pressure and no pipe is too fast, then takes back any size that was not needed. No pipe is made smaller. If the sizes you listed cannot meet the limits, it says so. For old pipes, name the options (name, new C, price) and a budget, then Choose pipes to rehabilitate. The budget is a ceiling, never exceeded; what it cannot reach is reported."}]};
